# fn64 WM2000 route: boot -> Exhibition -> Single Match -> IN-MATCH GAMEPLAY.
#
# As of 2026-08-07 this route reaches a live match: both wrestlers in the ring,
# a running match clock, and a controllable player 1. Every screen named in the
# comments below was read off a dumped frame, not inferred from what a wrestling
# menu "probably" does -- the representative frames are committed alongside in
# `reference/wm2000-frames/` and each section cites the one that evidences it.
#
# COMMITTED ON PURPOSE. The 420,000-step route that first reached WM2000's
# match-setup screen was written to /tmp, macOS reaped it, and that run is now
# unreproducible -- it survived only as prose in
# docs/BOOT-NOTES-WM2000.md:1812-1830. A route recipe is evidence; it belongs in
# the repository. That match-setup screen is no longer the deepest state
# reached: this file now goes past it into the match itself.
#
# Format (crates/fn64-boot-harness/src/controller_input_schedule.rs):
#   port first_read end_read buttons_hex stick_x stick_y
# The clock is the controller READ ORDINAL, not time or instructions: a read
# is a shared external boundary, so fn64 and a black-box emulator replay it
# identically even when host calls are substituted. Ranges are half-open and
# must not overlap per port. Roughly one read per game poll ~= one per frame.
#
# OSContPad.button bits: A=0x8000 B=0x4000 Z=0x2000 START=0x1000
#                        dU=0x0800 dD=0x0400 dL=0x0200 dR=0x0100
#
# Held inputs are avoided: WM2000's menus advance on edges, so a button held
# across a long read range registers once and then looks stuck. Every press
# below is one short range followed by a release range.
fn64.controller-input-schedule.v1

# --- Boot splash sequence (THQ / JAKKS / AKI). Idle; the logos are timed.
0    0   90 0x0000 0 0

# --- Title screen: START to enter the main menu.
0   90  100 0x1000 0 0
0  100  130 0x0000 0 0

# --- Main menu: A confirms Exhibition (the default highlighted entry).
0  130  140 0x8000 0 0
0  140  180 0x0000 0 0

# --- Match-type menu: A confirms Single Match.
0  180  190 0x8000 0 0
0  190  230 0x0000 0 0

# --- Player-count menu: "1P VS 2P" / "1P VS CPU" / "Watch". A takes the
#     default highlight (1P VS 2P), which is why the match below is a local
#     two-player card and NOT the CPU-opponent path. Observed 2026-08-07 in
#     `reference/wm2000-frames/` at gfx submit 350; the earlier comment here
#     called this a wrestler select, which it is not.
0  230  240 0x8000 0 0
0  240  280 0x0000 0 0
0  280  290 0x8000 0 0
0  290  330 0x0000 0 0

# --- Rules / options page: A accepts the defaults.
0  330  340 0x8000 0 0
0  340  380 0x0000 0 0

# --- Match-setup page ("New Belt" / "Non Title Match" / "Controller Pak",
#     with both wrestler slots EMPTY). A opens the Superstars roster.
0  380  390 0x8000 0 0
0  390  430 0x0000 0 0
0  430  440 0x1000 0 0
0  440  470 0x0000 0 0
0  470  480 0x8000 0 0
0  480  510 0x0000 0 0
0  510  520 0x1000 0 0
0  520  550 0x0000 0 0

# --- Superstars roster: STEVE AUSTIN / THE ROCK / TRIPLE H / MANKIND /
#     UNDERTAKER / KANE / BIG SHOW, with a 3D model preview and a
#     "STONE COLD 6'2" 252lbs" stat card.  See
#     `reference/wm2000-frames/wrestler-select-roster.png`.
#
#     THIS IS WHERE THE ROUTE USED TO DEAD-END, and the cause was not a
#     missing press -- it was a CANCEL. The old schedule sent B (0x4000) at
#     read 550 and Z (0x2000) at 590; `wrestler-select-cancelled.png` catches
#     the roster mid-animation sliding back out. The route reached the roster,
#     threw the selection away, and then idled 760 reads on the empty
#     match-setup screen. The old comment's claim that the arena loader "is
#     never asked for" was a true observation with the wrong diagnosis: no
#     wrestlers were ever confirmed, so there was no match to load.
#
#     A on the default highlight confirms Steve Austin for each slot.
0  550  560 0x8000 0 0
0  560  600 0x0000 0 0
0  600  610 0x8000 0 0
0  610  700 0x0000 0 0

# --- Decision page: Time Limit 30 Min., Pin/TKO/Submission/Rope Break Yes,
#     header now reading "STONE COLD / STONE COLD" -- both slots filled.
#     A commits it and the match loads. (`decision-screen.png`)
0  700  710 0x8000 0 0
0  710  900 0x0000 0 0

# --- Versus presentation: a LOOPING cinematic, not a timed one. Camera sweeps
#     the entrance rigging and cuts to the card "Single Match / STEVE AUSTIN
#     VS STEVE AUSTIN" (`versus-presentation.png`). Frame hashes repeat with
#     period ~600 submits, so idling here never exits -- that is the empirical
#     answer to the old "timed cutscene or input gate?" question. START skips
#     it; the A and second START clear the arena-load prompts behind it.
0  900  910 0x1000 0 0
0  910 1000 0x0000 0 0
0 1000 1010 0x8000 0 0
0 1010 1200 0x0000 0 0
0 1200 1210 0x1000 0 0
0 1210 1900 0x0000 0 0

# --- IN-MATCH GAMEPLAY. Both wrestlers are in the RAW IS WAR ring with the
#     crowd, two ATTITUDE meters, and a RUNNING MATCH CLOCK -- the clock is
#     the discriminator against a cutscene, and it advances 00:06 -> 00:17
#     across `in-match-clock-0006.png` and `in-match-clock-0017.png`.
#
#     These stick deflections prove the wrestler is CONTROLLABLE rather than
#     merely on screen: over the first range 1P walks left across the ring and
#     turns to face the opponent while the camera tracks him, and the ATTITUDE
#     meters diverge. Analog stick is x/y in the last two columns.
0 1900 1980 0x0000 80 0
0 1980 2060 0x0000 -80 0
0 2060 2070 0x8000 0 0
0 2070 2200 0x0000 0 0
0 2200 2280 0x0000 0 80
0 2280 3000 0x0000 0 0
